#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166960 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9dcbb0-d943-5340-916e-d789b6ab03bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Value to What a Fourier Series Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b796236-ecb8-5476-9123-68c2471bcce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b796236-ecb8-5476-9123-68c2471bcce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315549} :answer-field/id #uuid "66aeef22-5905-5dc6-b02a-1f2b21fdc21a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "883d77a7-ed55-5648-ad28-2c6a5e33fb4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-343359" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 3, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=3 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{3+(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166960 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9dcbb0-d943-5340-916e-d789b6ab03bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Value to What a Fourier Series Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bff08d19-8cb4-5226-967f-942523bd104b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bff08d19-8cb4-5226-967f-942523bd104b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316201} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf1a59b6-d6c2-5e99-8bc5-ed1d49da21ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "086a93d6-9edf-5e6c-82a6-6ae6a865d444" :question/math-academy-id "q-343370" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166961 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3cb7656-1ce6-5b6a-9e88-5ba24a943c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Infinite Sum Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8731841-813d-5c15-9844-bc300aded01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402286} {:answer/id #uuid "7d76bc51-9148-5c68-8eea-0c61f6eca229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402287} {:answer/id #uuid "4b83a754-c4bd-58d7-824f-3b576edd0dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0"