\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-3}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we separate the Fourier series into its odd and even terms as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)∼2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)x)-2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1}-2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n}\\cdot 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
